Work in a fixed order and keep the units:

- volume of usable rock $V = \text{area} \times \text{thickness}$ (in $\mathrm{m^3}$);
- mass $M = V \times \rho$, with $\rho$ in $\mathrm{t\,m^{-3}}$, giving tonnes;
- life of quarry $= M \div \text{annual output}$;
- stripping ratio $= \text{thickness of overburden} \div \text{thickness of rock}$ (or the same ratio of volumes when the area is the same);
- haulage cost per tonne $= \text{distance} \times \text{rate per tonne per km}$.

Then weigh the numbers against the qualitative factors: a site with the lowest cost on paper can still be rejected for unstable faces, flooding or objections from nearby residents.
